Q: I&#39;m trying to implement a view stack in swiftui and my @State objects are being reset for reasons that are unclear to me A: <p>I can't say for sure how <code>NavigationView</code> (now deprecated) or <code>NavigationStack</code> are actually implemented, but I can explain why your approach didn't work.</p>
<p>Your attempt at building a navigation stack doesn't work because of two SwiftUI behaviors interacting:</p>
<ol>
<li><p>You're creating the <code>AAA</code> view in your <code>ContentView.init</code> and storing it in <code>_stack</code> before SwiftUI has ever seen the <code>AAA</code> view, before SwiftUI has a chance to allocate storage for the <code>_data</code> property of the <code>AAA</code> view and update the <code>_data</code> property to point to that storage.</p>
<p>The way you get SwiftUI to “see” a view and deal with its <code>State</code> properties is by returning the view from some other view's (or <code>Scene</code>'s) <code>body</code> property.</p>
</li>
<li><p>In <code>ContentView.body</code>, you do return a copy of that <code>AAA</code> view to SwiftUI. (All views are passed/returned as copies, because all views are value types—<code>struct</code>s or <code>enum</code>s. If you try to make a <code>class</code> or <code>actor</code> conform to <code>View</code>, you get a compile-time error.) SwiftUI does allocate storage for the <code>AAA</code> view after that, and updates the copy's <code>_data</code> to point at the storage.</p>
<p>Alas, when the Push button is pressed, you remove the <code>AAA</code> view from the view hierarchy. Nothing is left to hold on to the allocated storage, so it gets deallocated.</p>
<p>When you put an <code>AAA</code> back in the view hierarchy later (when the Pop button is pressed), SwiftUI has to allocate fresh storage for it.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>So how does <code>NavigationStack</code> (or <code>NavigationView</code>) deal with these problems? It is built in to SwiftUI and so it has access to lots of implementation details that we third-party schlubs can't use. The built-in navigation containers almost certainly use internal APIs to preserve the persistent state of hidden views.</p>
<p>Here is one way you can work around the problem. You need to put the <code>AAA</code> in <code>_stack</code> <strong>after</strong> SwiftUI has mucked around with its <code>State</code> properties. That modified version stored in <code>_stack</code> will hold on to the allocated storage, and SwiftUI will use that existing storage when you put <code>AAA</code> back in the view hierarchy.</p>
<p>You have access to that modified version of <code>AAA</code> when SwiftUI asks it for its <code>body</code> property. So you have access to it when you call the <code>push</code> function in <code>AAA</code>'s <code>body</code>. Let's take advantage of that.</p>
<p>Change your <code>push</code> function to take two arguments: the pusher and the pushee:</p>
<pre><code>typealias Push = (_ pusher: AnyView, _ pushee: AnyView) -&gt; ()
struct PushKey: EnvironmentKey {
    static let defaultValue: Push = { _, _ in }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Change <code>ContentView</code>'s <code>push</code> method to replace the last stack entry with the pusher before pushing the pushee:</p>
<pre><code>    public func push(pusher: AnyView, pushee: AnyView) {
      stack.removeLast()
      stack.append(pusher)
      stack.append(pushee)
    }
</code></pre>
<p>Finally, pass <code>self</code> to the <code>push</code> function in <code>AAA</code>'s <code>Button</code> action:</p>
<pre><code>struct AAA : View {
    @State private var data = &quot;default text&quot;
    @Environment(\.push) var push

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            TextEditor(text: $data)
            Button(&quot;Push&quot;) {
                self.push(AnyView(self), AnyView(BBB()))
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to check if a function is a constructor A: <p>In the old days you just had to test for functions with a prototype property:</p>
<pre><code>typeof value=='function' &amp;&amp; 'prototype' in value// function with prototype property
</code></pre>
<p>But those were the days before bound and generator functions. For example the above will return <code>false</code> for constructors created with <code>Function.prototype.bind()</code> and <code>true</code> for generator functions (which can't construct per the ES7 spec).</p>
<p>So these days there are better ways, like this cross-browser solution that also works with bound and generator functions. Acknowledgement to Ryan Hanekamp for his answer which I incorporated in my answer below. This works in both modern and old ES3+ browsers including Node.js and IE:</p>
<pre><code>var isConstructor = typeof Proxy=='function'?
function(value) {// Works in modern environments that support Proxy (ES6+)
    if (typeof value=='function') {// Test for [[Construct]] internal method
        try {// Throws if `value` is not a constructor function
            var success = new new Proxy(value, {construct:Object});
        }
        catch(e) {
        }
    }
    return !!success
}:
function(value) {// Tested in pre-ES6 JS including Internet Explorer 6 and up

    // Test for bound functions and functions with a prototype property
    // That also applies to generators since they construct in pre-ES7 JS
    var is_function = typeof value=='function';

    // In IE, host objects with a prototype property can construct too.
    // So it doesn't only apply to objects where typeof value=='function'
    var has_proto_prop = value===Object(value) &amp;&amp; 'prototype' in value;

    // If the function has a prototype property and is not a built-in
    // function starting with lowercase, it is probably a construtor.
    // RegExp needed since in IE: !!alert.prototype// true for host functions
    // For IE6 host constructors: value!==value// true
    var success = is_function? has_proto_prop &amp;&amp; !/^\s*\w+ [a-z_]\w*\(\) {\s*\[native c/.test(value): value!==value &amp;&amp; typeof value=='object';

    // In IE, value could be a constructor where typeof value!='function'
    // Or no prototype property means value might be a bound constructor
    if (has_proto_prop!=is_function) {
        try {
            // instanceof can be used to test for constructors because it
            // throws when used on functions with no prototype property.
            // With bound functions it uses prototype property of target.
            // False positives can occur if bound function's target isn't
            // a constructor but has a prototype property like IE's alert()
            success = {} instanceof value || true
        }
        catch(e) {
        }
    }
    return success
}
isConstructor(window)//false
isConstructor(alert)//false
isConstructor(XMLHttpRequest)//true
isConstructor(Array.bind())//true
isConstructor(function myFunc(){})//true
isConstructor(function* myGenerator(){})//false
// Caveat: IE returns false-positives on bound host objects
isConstructor(isNaN.bind())//false (works in IE since isNaN isn't a host object)
isConstructor(alert.bind())//false (however in IE this returns true (false-positive))
</code></pre>
